@page "/recipes/grid-from-database"

<PageTitle>A data grid from a database · Trailblazor</PageTitle>

<DocsArticle Class="docs-article--guide">
    <Contents>
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="the-code" Text="The code" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="how-it-works" Text="How it works" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="related" Text="Related" />
    </Contents>
    <Header>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Headline" As="TextElement.H1">A data grid from a database</TbText>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" class="docs-lead">
            Turn what a grid asks for into an EF Core query, so only one page of rows leaves the database, and reload
            the grid after the data changes.
        </TbText>
    </Header>
    <ChildContent>
        <DocsHeading Id="the-code" Text="The code" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            This site has no database, so this page shows the code without running it. The two files compile with the
            site, so they keep up with the library. <a href="components/data-grid#server-data">Rows from a server</a>
            has a similar grid you can try.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="HikeLog.razor" Code="@HikeLogSource" />
        <CodeBlock Label="HikeContext.cs" Code="@HikeContextSource" />
        <CodeBlock Label="Program.cs" Code="@Registration" />

        <DocsHeading Id="how-it-works" Text="How it works" Class="docs-section" />
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>DataProvider</code> gets a <code>GridRequest</code> with the filters, the sorts, and which rows to
                give. It answers with those rows and the count of all the rows that pass the filters.
            </li>
            <li>
                Each filter and sort becomes part of the query, so the database does the work. The request identifies
                each column by its field name, and <code>nameof</code> keeps those names in step with the class.
            </li>
            <li>
                If the query can't apply a filter or sort, it throws, so the grid shows that the load failed instead of
                seeming to ignore a control. Add a case for each column you make sortable or filterable.
            </li>
            <li>
                A <code>Contains</code> here follows the database's collation, so it can be case-sensitive, where a grid
                given <code>Items</code> ignores case. Check how your database compares text.
            </li>
            <li>
                The id comes last in the sort. Without it, rows with the same values can change places between pages,
                and a row can show twice or not at all.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>RefreshAsync</code> loads the rows again after a save, because the grid can't tell that the
                database changed.
            </li>
            <li>
                When a load fails, the grid says so and offers to try again, but it doesn't log anything, so
                <code>OnLoadFailed</code> logs the exception. A failed save is the page's own to catch, since it would
                otherwise end a server page's connection.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>IDbContextFactory</code> gives each load a context of its own, since a grid can ask for new rows
                while the last load is still running. Register it with your database's provider, as
                <code>Program.cs</code> shows for SQL Server, which needs the
                <code>Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer</code> package.
            </li>
            <li>
                The token passed to <code>DataProvider</code> cancels the query when a newer request replaces it.
            </li>
            <li>
                EF Core needs to run on the server. On a WebAssembly page, have <code>DataProvider</code> call an API that runs
                this query, and send it the request's filters, sorts, start index and count.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <DocsHeading Id="related" Text="Related" Class="docs-section" />
        <ul>
